{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`caused_by` is the only machine-readable causation edge in the store, and it is not\ntrustworthy today. `rebar link <bug> <target> caused_by` accepts ANY target: nothing checks\nthat the target is a change at all. The most-linked target in the current store is a\nmisattribution whose \"children\" are unrelated CI items, so `rebar metrics`\n(`bug_caused_by_fan_in`) and the epic-close `caused_by` floor (`llm/epic_bug_screen.py`)\nboth reason over edges nobody validated.\n\nVerified against the tree at `origin/main` before writing this plan:\n\n* `rebar link` validates only relation grammar, cycles, the redundant-link guard and the\n  closed-source guard (`graph/_links.py:add_dependency`), and has NO `--force`\n  (`_cli/_parsers/core/writes.py:build_link` exposes only `--dry-run`).\n* The \"does a commit reference this ticket?\" scan exists exactly once, privately, as\n  `_referencing_commits` in `_commands/close_precheck.py`. It walks `git log` and resolves\n  each `rebar-ticket:`/subject ref through the shared resolver. Its `[]` return conflates\n  \"scanned, none found\" with \"no git history\".\n* The bug-close path (`transition_close._apply_caused_by`) writes its blame-derived edge via\n  `graph._links._write_link_event`, deliberately bypassing `add_dependency`; it is\n  best-effort and out of scope here.\n\n## Approach\n\nOne shared scan, two consumers — the epic's shared-module shape.\n\n1. Hoist the trailer/subject scan into the existing shared module\n   `_engine_support/commit_impact.py` as `referencing_commits(ids, tracker, repo_root)`,\n   returning `list[str] | None` where `None` means \"git history could not be read\" — the\n   same `None`-vs-`[]` distinction `changed_paths` in that module already uses.\n   `close_precheck._referencing_commits` becomes a thin `... or []` delegate KEEPING ITS\n   NAME (it is a monkeypatch target by string in `tests/unit/test_attach_commits.py`, so\n   the name is a contract), and `_referencing_commit_exists` keeps delegating through it.\n2. Refuse an unverifiable `caused_by` at link time in `composer.link_core`\n   (`_commands/link_revert.py`) — the ONE seam library, CLI and MCP all route through, so\n   parity is structural rather than triplicated. The rule: the RESOLVED TARGET must have at\n   least one commit referencing it. Targeting the ticket itself (not its descendants) is\n   deliberate: `caused_by` should name the specific change that introduced the defect, and\n   crediting an epic's children is the misattribution shape this story exists to stop.\n   `caused_by` is never hierarchy-promoted, so the resolved target is the requested one.\n   NOT in `add_dependency`: `rebar.graph` is the store layer and today runs NO git and takes\n   no `repo_root` (only `tracker_dir`); `link_core` is the command-layer seam that already\n   receives `repo_root` and is the only functional caller of `add_dependency`. `link_core`\n   resolves the CODE root as `config.repo_root(repo_root)` and passes THAT — never the\n   tracker dir, which an absolute `tracker.dir` can relocate outside the checkout — exactly\n   mirroring close_precheck's `code_root`/`tracker` split.\n3. Unverifiable is NOT invalid: when the scan returns `None` (no code repo / no commits) the\n   link is allowed. A refusal must mean \"this target has no commit\", never \"this clone has\n   no history\".\n4. Escape hatch `--force[=<reason>]` on the CLI, `force=` on library and MCP, spelled\n   exactly as `claim --force`. Its parser is hoisted to\n   `_cli/_parsers/core/writes.py:extract_force` and `claim._parse_force` delegates to it, so\n   the spelling has one implementation rather than a third copy. `link --dry-run` previews\n   the refusal instead of \"Would create\".\n5. Write-parity oracle extensions AC-1 needs, named explicitly: a `\"link\"` branch in\n   `write_parity_contract.execute()` (and in the `Case.op` vocabulary), plus a `link` method\n   on the Adapter protocol and all three adapters changed from `(a, b, relation) -> None`\n   to `(a, b, relation, *, force=None) -> Outcome`, so a refusal/force row can be classified.\n   No existing test calls `adapter.link`, so the signature change has no other consumer.\n\n## Scope\n\n`src/rebar/_engine_support/commit_impact.py`, `src/rebar/_commands/close_precheck.py`,\n`src/rebar/_commands/link_revert.py`, `src/rebar/_cli/_parsers/core/writes.py`,\n`src/rebar/_cli/help/link.txt`, `src/rebar/_commands/claim.py`,\n`src/rebar/_lib_mutations.py`, `src/rebar/_mcp_writes.py`,\n`tests/unit/test_caused_by_link_validation.py` (new), `tests/interfaces/adapters.py`,\n`tests/interfaces/write_parity_contract.py`, `docs/ticket-model.md`,\n`examples/agent-skills/rebar-debug/SKILL.md`.\n\n## Explicitly OUT of scope\n\nThe original plan's `introducing_sha` schema field + `rebar fsck` date check, and the\n\"attach the 196-row dated corpus\" criterion, are cut and re-filed as a follow-up linked\n`discovered_from`. Reasons: (a) no surface writes `introducing_sha`, so the field would land\ndead; (b) `fsck` is a STORE-integrity walker that must run where no code repo exists, so a\ngit-history check is the wrong home; (c) the corpus is a review artifact absent from the repo.\n\n## Testing\n\nNew `tests/unit/test_caused_by_link_validation.py` (store with real commits):\n\n* happy path — `caused_by` to a target carrying a `rebar-ticket:` trailer commit succeeds;\n* refusal — `caused_by` to a commit-less target raises, names the target and `--force`, and\n  writes NO edge (`show_ticket(...)[\"deps\"]` unchanged);\n* `force=\"...\"` on the same input succeeds and records the edge;\n* other relations (`relates_to`, `blocks`) to the same commit-less target are unaffected;\n* unverifiable — a store whose code repo has no commits allows the link;\n* `link --dry-run` on a commit-less target prints a \"Would reject\" preview and writes nothing;\n* delegation — `close_precheck._referencing_commits` still returns `[]`, not `None`, when\n  history is unreadable, and is still resolvable by that name.\n\nPinning (must pass unchanged): `tests/unit/test_attach_commits.py` (the monkeypatch target),\n`tests/unit/test_caused_by_dedup_10d0.py`, `tests/unit/test_epic_bug_screen_4b54.py`,\n`tests/unit/test_close_gate_*.py`, `tests/unit/test_claim_*.py`,\n`tests/interfaces/test_write_parity_oracle.py`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A `caused_by` link to a commit-less target is refused identically on library, CLI and\n      MCP, and `force` bypasses it on all three; checked by two new `op=\"link\"` rows in the\n      write-parity oracle: `pytest tests/interfaces/test_write_parity_oracle.py -q`.\n- [ ] Refusal names the target and the escape hatch and leaves the store unwritten; the\n      unverifiable-store case is allowed; `link --dry-run` previews the refusal; checked by\n      `pytest tests/unit/test_caused_by_link_validation.py -q`.\n- [ ] The scan has ONE implementation and the close gate is behaviour-identical; checked by\n      `pytest tests/unit/test_attach_commits.py tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_completion_gate.py -q`\n      plus `grep -c 'git.*log.*%H' src/rebar/_commands/close_precheck.py` returning 0 and\n      `grep -c '_referencing_commits' src/rebar/_commands/close_precheck.py` returning >0.\n- [ ] Gates green: `make lint && make typecheck && python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check`\n      reports `new=0 increased=0`.\n"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787414572738628001,"uuid":"feec4340-1c75-4c35-ad4d-29e3c136ec61"}